.env
# Generated by test.py — build/score artifacts, same status as data/
test/

# Generated by calc.py — scored-submission artifacts, same status as data/
calc/

# Per-cell-type task fixtures pulled from /api/v3/tasks by search_hdr.py and the offline harnesses.
# Scratch inputs, not source: each is one round's contract + hbb_reference, refetched on demand.
# The hyphen matters — task.json is a tracked fixture and must stay so.
task-*.json

# CPU torch venv for seed_model/ (865 MB). Deliberately separate from .venv so the miner runtime
# never sees torch — see seed_model/README.md. The `.venv` rule above does not cover this name.
.venv-ml/

# seed_model checkpoints. Rewritten by every promoted refresh cycle, which round_plan.sh runs
# whenever a round stamps (~10/day), so tracking them means a ~6 MB binary diff per cycle for a
# file nothing reads back. seed_model/state.json is the durable record of the walk-forward score;
# a fresh clone regenerates the weights with `python -m seed_model.train --refresh` in ~65 s.
seed_model/**/*.pt

# Written by seed_watch.py — one JSON record per poll, appended forever while the watcher runs
# (30s x 3 tasks x 24h is ~2 MB/day, and it was already 1.1 MB when this rule was added). It is a
# measurement LOG, not an input: nothing reads it back, and what it establishes gets summarised into
# CLAUDE.md by hand. Tracking an append-only file means a whole new blob in every commit for content
# no clone needs. The script itself stays tracked — it is the reproducible part.
seed_watch.jsonl

# Research-harness output. The repo root is where ~40 offline harnesses drop their result JSON
# (`conj_*.json`, `win20_*.json`, `sd_sweep_*.json`, `validate_*.json`, ...) and 341 of them had
# accumulated as tracked files totalling 37 MB — `scores_fresh.json` alone is 30 MB. None is an
# input to anything: each is one run's numbers, and what those numbers ESTABLISH is written up in
# CLAUDE.md by hand, which is the durable record. Re-running the harness regenerates the file.
#
# **2026-09-21: UNANCHORED by operator request — `*.json` now matches at every depth**, not just
# the repo root. What that buys is `reports/`: 925 untracked JSON had accumulated there (one
# `reports/<task>/<hotkey>/stage*_detail.seed*.json` set per scored round) and every new round added
# more. What it does NOT do is untrack the 780 `*.json` already in the index — 741 of them in
# `reports/` — because gitignore only applies to UNTRACKED paths. So `reports/` is now half tracked
# and half ignored; `git rm -r --cached reports/` is the other half of that job if the old ones
# should go too.
#
# The negations below matter more than they did when this rule was anchored. Unanchored, `*.json`
# reaches `seed_model/`, `.claude/` and `.vscode/`, and `seed_model/` holds real INPUTS —
# `seeds.json` is read by 9 .py files, `walk_record.json` by 7 (it is the only source for the
# `model` strategy's per-task history, which cannot be recomputed without torch), and
# `next_prediction.json` by 5. Without the negation a new seed_model input would be ignored by
# default, which is the mirror image of the accumulation problem this rule was written for.
# Add a `!` line for anything that is genuinely an input.
*.json
# ...except these, which are read AS INPUTS or are durable records, not outputs.
#
# **Comments go on their OWN line here.** gitignore has no trailing-comment syntax: a `#` after a
# pattern is part of the pattern, so `!/task.json   # a fixture` is the literal pattern
# `/task.json   # a fixture` and matches nothing. The five negations below carried trailing
# comments from the day they were written and were therefore ALL dead — they appeared to work only
# because every one of those files is tracked, and gitignore never applies to a tracked path. Check
# a negation with `git check-ignore -v --no-index <path>`; without `--no-index` git skips tracked
# paths and reports "not ignored" whatever the patterns say, which is exactly what hid this.

# a tracked fixture — see the `task-*.json` rule above, which spares it too
!/task.json
# /api/v3/tasks cache, read by ~25 harnesses via sd_task.fetch(cache=...)
!/sd_task_listing.json
# /api/v3/miners/scores cache, read by ~15 harnesses the same way
!/sd_task_scores.json
# the accruing shadow log of window predictions, read by window_plan.py
!/seed_window_log.json
# coldkey grouping, read as input by mingap.py / mingap_cells.py
!/hotkey_coldkey.json
# seed_model inputs: seeds.json (9 .py), walk_record.json (7), next_prediction.json (5)
!seed_model/*.json
# project settings
!.claude/*.json
# package SOURCE — a JSON shipped inside the package is data, not harness output
!niome_subnet/**/*.json
# NO `!.vscode/*.json`: extensions.json is already tracked so gitignore cannot touch it, and a
# negation here would sit AFTER the deliberate `.vscode/settings.json` rule above and undo it.

# The same rule for the other two formats the report harnesses emit, added 2026-09-21 by operator
# request. `reports/<task>/<hotkey>/validation.txt` is calc.py's printed five-stage report and
# `reports/<task>/summary.csv` is render_report.py's table — one set per scored round, so they grow
# at the same rate as the JSON above and for the same reason.
#
# **No negations, and that is checked rather than assumed**: every .csv in the repo is opened `"w"`
# by a `render_report.py` and never read back, nothing in *.sh or *.toml references either
# extension, and there is no requirements.txt (see the Commands section of CLAUDE.md — the docs
# that mention one are wrong). If that changes, add a `!` line on its OWN line — see the note above
# about trailing comments silently killing a negation.
*.csv
*.txt
# The one defensive negation: there is no requirements.txt today (uv + pyproject.toml), but it is
# the classic file a blanket `*.txt` swallows, and a silently-ignored one breaks a fresh clone.
!requirements.txt
